Chapter: FsServicer
  cites Foreword chapter FileSystem

 BACKLOG 1.14: the kernel services the FileSystem effect, and a program
 that holds only FileSystem and never Device.Block can now read and write
 the disk through it.

 This program declares FileSystem.Read and FileSystem.Write scoped to "OK"
 and NOTHING else -- no Device.Block. It performs write-file and read-text
 as bare effect ops with no `with` of its own, so the compiler's default
 handler services them: a hand-emitted stub checks the granted direction
 bit, raises the kernel fs-elevated cell so the block device answers even
 though this program holds no Device.Block, drives fat16, and lowers the
 cell. Before this existed a bare read-text/write-file had no handler at
 all and dereferenced a null slot; the guarantee it makes real is that
 holding FileSystem read/write is enough to reach the disk, and holding
 Device.Block is not required to.

 The write lands and the read-back returns its bytes, which is the whole
 claim: the elevation reached the disk on behalf of a program that holds
 no Device.Block. A servicer that forgot to elevate would fail the write
 (the block syscall would refuse a process without cap-block-device); one
 that never installed the default handler would dereference a null slot
 and crash. The out-of-scope case is not tested here because it cannot
 compile: write-file is an effect op, so an out-of-scope literal is caught
 at compile time (CDX4002), and the runtime path scope through fat16 is
 pinned by scope-runtime-deny.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, FileSystem.Read "OK", FileSystem.Write "OK"] Nothing
  opening = act
    wrote <- write-file "OK.TXT" "hello servicer"
    print-line-uni ("write " & show wrote)

    back <- read-text "OK.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("read " & back)
  end
